# Can two people trade their way to efficiency?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does the Edgeworth box show about where trade can end up? Why are there many efficient outcomes, not one?"
next: {"title":"What does the first welfare theorem prove — and what doesn't it?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/25-what-does-the-first-welfare-theorem.cast"}

## The same parcel
sources:
    - id: radford1945
      title: The Economic Organisation of a P.O.W. Camp
      authors: R. A. Radford
      year: 1945
      finding: 'In POW camps with equal Red Cross parcels, barter began at once (a non-smoker''s cigarettes for a smoker''s chocolate), exchange rates formed within a week or two, and cigarettes became currency; ''it is by trade that individual preferences are given expression and comfort increased.'' Economica 12(48): 189–201.'

    icon p_l size 170 x 250 y 500 or ["person"] icon_look picture of man hidden true
    icon p_r size 170 x 750 y 500 or ["person"] icon_look picture of man hidden true
    icon choc_l size 88 x 92.5 y 340 icon_look picture of "chocolate bar" hidden true
    icon cig_l size 88 set openmoji x 197.5 y 340 icon_look picture of cigarette hidden true
    icon can_l size 88 x 302.5 y 340 icon_look picture of "canned food" hidden true
    icon jar_l size 88 x 407.5 y 340 icon_look picture of jar hidden true
    icon choc_r size 88 x 592.5 y 340 icon_look picture of "chocolate bar" hidden true
    icon cig_r size 88 set openmoji x 697.5 y 340 icon_look picture of cigarette hidden true
    icon can_r size 88 x 802.5 y 340 icon_look picture of "canned food" hidden true
    icon jar_r size 88 x 907.5 y 340 icon_look picture of jar hidden true
    text same "same parcel" #8f887c x 500 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    text lab_l "non-smoker" #2f6b8f x 250 y 270 font_size 32 hidden true
    text lab_r "smoker" #b5482e x 750 y 270 font_size 32 hidden true
    text camp "POW camp, 1943–45" #8f887c x 500 y 610 font_size 30 hidden true
    text beef "tinned beef  →" x 360 y 200 font_size 32 hidden true
    text beef2 "jam, margarine" x 660 y 200 font_size 32 hidden true
    text quote "“it is by trade that individual preferences\nare given expression and comfort increased”\n— R. A. Radford, 1945" cites ["radford1945"] x 500 y 250 font_size 28 hidden true
    text stop "Where does swapping stop?" #b5482e x 500 y 215 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "The same parcel"

Can two people, only by swapping what they already have, reach a deal that can't be improved? Start in a prisoner-of (@draw camp@)-war camp during the Second World War.
    draw p_l p_r parallel true

B: Every prisoner got the same R (@draw same@)ed Cross parcel: tinned milk, jam, biscuits, chocolate, cigarettes.
    draw choc_l cig_l can_l jar_l choc_r cig_r can_r jar_r parallel true

B: My guess: nobody would bother trading. Everyone holds exactly the same things.
    highlight same

    quiz "Everyone holds exactly the same parcel. Do they trade?" judge false
        right: A whole market sprang up. Within a week or two there were going rates for jam, beef and cigarettes.
        * Hardly: nobody has anything new
        * A little, between friends
        * A lot: a whole market

    erase same

It began simply: a non-smoker gave a smoker friend  (@move cig_l by [395,0] duration 1.4@)his cigarettes for  (@move choc_r by [-395,0] duration 1.4@)the friend's chocolate.
    draw lab_l lab_r parallel true

B: And both walked away happier, though nothing new was made. The same things just changed hands.
    highlight choc_l cig_l choc_r cig_r effect circle

Sikh prisoners at first swapped their tinned beef for almost any other food, until they learne (@draw beef2@)d to ask for jam and margarine.
    draw beef

    erase beef beef2

B: A young economist in the camp, R. A. Radford, wrote it up in 1945: everyone go (@draw quote@)t a roughly equal share, and it was by trade that individual preferences were given expression and comfort increased.
    erase lab_l lab_r

    erase quote

So swapping alone makes people better off. Wh (@draw stop@)ere does it stop, and is there one best place to stop?
    highlight p_l p_r

B: To answer that, we need one picture that holds two people's things at once.
    fade p_l p_r choc_l choc_r cig_l cig_r can_l can_r jar_l jar_r camp to 0.3

## The box
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 10
    'y':
      - 0
      - 10
    box:
      x: 150
      'y': 168
      w: 475
      h: 475

    curve edge_b #3d3833 expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_t #3d3833 expr "10" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_l #3d3833 x_expr "0" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_r #3d3833 x_expr "10" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    dot oM #2f6b8f at.x 0 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_oM "Mia" below-left #2f6b8f attach_to oM hidden true
    dot oF #b5482e at.x 10 at.y 10 hidden true
    label l_oF "Finn" above-right #b5482e attach_to oF hidden true
    text t_mf "Mia's fish →" #2f6b8f at.data [5,-0.75] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_mb "Mia's\nloaves ↑" #2f6b8f at.data [-1.3,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_ff "← Finn's fish" #b5482e at.data [5,10.7] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_fb "Finn's\nloaves ↓" #b5482e at.data [11.3,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot E #3d3833 at.x 2 at.y 8 hidden true
    label l_E "start (2, 8)" above-right attach_to E hidden true
    text hold_m "Mia: 2 fish, 8 loaves" #2f6b8f x 815 y 560 font_size 26 hidden true
    text hold_f "Finn: 8 fish, 2 loaves" #b5482e x 815 y 515 font_size 26 hidden true
    curve mia_E thick #2f6b8f expr 16/x x_from 1.6 x_to 10 hidden true
    text l_miaE "Mia's curve" #2f6b8f at.data [11.5,1.2] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve finn_E thick #b5482e expr 10-16/(10-x) x_from 0 x_to 8.4 hidden true
    text l_finnE "Finn's curve" #b5482e at.data [8.9,-0.75] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot pA #8f887c at.x 3 at.y 9 hidden true
    label lA "A" right #8f887c attach_to pA hidden true
    group gA members ["pA","lA"] hidden true
    dot pB #8f887c at.x 5 at.y 5 hidden true
    label lB "B" right #8f887c attach_to pB hidden true
    group gB members ["pB","lB"] hidden true
    dot pC #8f887c at.x 7 at.y 1.5 hidden true
    label lC "C" right #8f887c attach_to pC hidden true
    group gC members ["pC","lC"] hidden true
    region lens x_from 2 x_to 8 between ["finn_E","mia_E"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text t_lens "gold lens:\nboth gain" #b8860b x 830 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow trade #3d3833 from.x 2.2 from.y 7.8 to.x 4.8 to.y 5.2 hidden true
    text t_deal "3 loaves → Finn\n3 fish → Mia" x 830 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "The box"

B: Take two people in Harbourtown, with illustrative numbers: Mia the baker and Finn, a fisher. Between them they have ten fish and ten loaves.
    draw edge_b edge_l edge_t edge_r

B: This box holds all of it. Mia counts from the bottom-left corner: fish to the right, loaves up.
    draw oM l_oM t_mf t_mb

And Finn?
    draw oF l_oF

B: From the top-right corner, upside down: his  (@draw t_fb@)fish run to the left, his loaves down. So every point in th (@highlight edge_b edge_l edge_t edge_r effect box@)e box splits all twenty things between them.
    draw t_ff

B: They start here. Mia has two f (@draw hold_m@)ish and eight loaves; Finn has eigh (@draw hold_f@)t fish and two loaves.
    draw E l_E

So she's rich in bread and he's rich in fish. What does each of them care about?
    highlight hold_m hold_f

B: Mia's curve through the start joins every split she likes exactly as much. Anything above it, she prefers.
    draw mia_E l_miaE

B: Finn's curve through the same point bows the other way. He prefers anything below it, further from his own corner.
    draw finn_E l_finnE

    erase hold_m hold_f

Here are three deals they could strike. Only one makes both better off.
    draw gA gB gC

    ask "Which deal leaves both Mia and Finn better off than at the start? Tap it." choose ["gA","gB","gC"] answer gB store d
        right: B: it sits above Mia's curve and below Finn's.
        wrong: Look for the deal above Mia's blue curve and, at the same time, below Finn's red one.

B: B. Every deal between the two curves helps both (@draw t_lens@) of them: this lens.
    draw lens

Say they settle at five and fi (@draw trade@)ve: Mia hands over three (@draw t_deal@) loaves for three of Finn's fish. Both end above where they began.
    erase gA gC

## Where trade stops
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 10
    'y':
      - 0
      - 10
    box:
      x: 150
      'y': 168
      w: 475
      h: 475
vars:
    eF:
      value: 2
      min: 0.5
      max: 9.5
    eB:
      value: 8
      min: 0.5
      max: 9.5
    finn_fish:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0.1
      max: 0.9
      step: 0.05
      decimals: 2
    p:
      value: 5
      min: 1
      max: 9
    k:
      expr: (1-finn_fish)/finn_fish
    pb:
      expr: 10*p/(k*(10-p)+p)
    uE:
      expr: eF*eB
    vE:
      expr: (10-eF)^finn_fish*(10-eB)^(1-finn_fish)
    uP:
      expr: p*pb
    vP:
      expr: (10-p)^finn_fish*(10-pb)^(1-finn_fish)
sources:
    - id: edgeworth1881
      title: Mathematical Psychics
      authors: F. Y. Edgeworth
      year: 1881
      finding: Introduced the contract curve of efficient exchanges between two traders and argued that 'contract without competition is indeterminate'; with more traders the range of final settlements shrinks (proved by Debreu and Scarf, 1963).

    curve edge_b #3d3833 expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_t #3d3833 expr "10" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_l #3d3833 x_expr "0" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_r #3d3833 x_expr "10" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    dot oM #2f6b8f at.x 0 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_oM "Mia" below-left #2f6b8f attach_to oM hidden true
    dot oF #b5482e at.x 10 at.y 10 hidden true
    label l_oF "Finn" above-right #b5482e attach_to oF hidden true
    text t_mf "Mia's fish →" #2f6b8f at.data [5,-0.75] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_mb "Mia's\nloaves ↑" #2f6b8f at.data [-1.3,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_ff "← Finn's fish" #b5482e at.data [5,10.7] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_fb "Finn's\nloaves ↓" #b5482e at.data [11.3,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve mia_E #2f6b8f expr (uE/x)+0*sqrt((uE/x)*(10-(uE/x))) x_from 0.05 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve finn_E #b5482e expr (10-(vE/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish)))+0*sqrt((10-(vE/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish)))*(10-(10-(vE/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish))))) x_from 0 x_to 9.95 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve lens_top expr max(uE/x,10-(vE/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish))) x_from 0.05 x_to 9.95 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    region lens between ["lens_top","mia_E"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    dot E #3d3833 at.x 2 at.y 8 bind {"at.x":"eF","at.y":"eB"} hidden true
    label l_E "start" above-right attach_to E hidden true
    dot D #3d3833 at.x 5 at.y 5 bind {"at.x":"p","at.y":"pb"} hidden true
    label l_D "deal" below-right attach_to D hidden true
    curve mia_P thick #2f6b8f expr (uP/x)+0*sqrt((uP/x)*(10-(uP/x))) x_from 0.05 x_to 10 hidden true
    curve finn_P thick #b5482e expr (10-(vP/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish)))+0*sqrt((10-(vP/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish)))*(10-(10-(vP/(10-x)^finn_fish)^(1/(1-finn_fish))))) x_from 0 x_to 9.95 hidden true
    curve tan_D dashed #8f887c expr (pb-(pb/p)*(x-p))+0*sqrt((pb-(pb/p)*(x-p))*(10-(pb-(pb/p)*(x-p)))) x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    curve tan_E dashed #8f887c expr (eB-(eB/eF)*(x-eF))+0*sqrt((eB-(eB/eF)*(x-eF))*(10-(eB-(eB/eF)*(x-eF)))) x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    text t_eff "curves touch,\nsame slope:\nefficient" x 830 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    curve cc #8a5fa8 expr 10*x/(k*(10-x)+x) x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 3.5 hidden true
    text l_cc "contract curve" #8a5fa8 at.data [4.8,1.8] font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait edgeworth x 840 y 430 width 190 of "Francis Ysidro Edgeworth" hidden true
    curve core #b8860b expr x x_from 4 x_to 6 style.stroke_width 9 hidden true
    text t_core "trade ends\nsomewhere here" #b8860b x 830 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t_ind "“contract without\ncompetition is\nindeterminate”\n— Edgeworth, 1881" cites ["edgeworth1881"] x 830 y 260 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_eq "equal parcels\n5 fish, 5 loaves" x 830 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_taste "Finn likes fish\nless than Mia" #b5482e x 830 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Where trade stops"

    draw edge_b edge_l edge_t edge_r oM l_oM oF l_oF t_mf t_mb t_ff t_fb lens_top mia_E finn_E E l_E lens

The lens holds every swap that helps both. Once they're at five and five, can they do better still?
    draw D l_D

    fade mia_E finn_E lens to 0.35

B: Draw each one's cu (@draw tan_D@)rve through the new deal. They just touch.
    draw mia_P finn_P

So no lens is left. Any swap from here that helps Mia hurts Finn: the deal is Pareto efficient.
    camera center.ref D zoom 2.2 duration 1.2

    camera reset true duration 0.8

B: So five and five is the answer?
    draw t_eff

    erase t_eff

One answer of many. Move the deal along, and at every point the two curves touch again.
    animate p 3 duration 2.2

    animate p 7.5 duration 2.5

Join the touching points and you get a line from Mia's corner to Finn's. (@draw edgeworth@) Francis Edgeworth, in 1881, called it  (@draw l_cc@)the contract curve.
    draw cc

    erase edgeworth

B: Even its two ends, where one of them has everything, are efficient. Efficient means nothing is wasted, not that it's fair.
    highlight oM oF effect circle

    erase mia_P finn_P D l_D tan_D

    fade mia_E finn_E lens to 1

From their start, though, trade can only reach this (@draw t_core@) stretch, inside the lens. Neither signs a deal worse than where they began.
    draw core

B: And where on the stretch they land depends on who bargains harder. Edgeworth's verdict: contract without competition is indeterminate.
    draw t_ind

    erase core t_core t_ind

Now back to the prison camp. Give them equal parcels, five of each (@draw t_eq@), and the same tastes: the start sits on the (@draw tan_E@) curve, and there's nothing to trade.
    animate eF 5 eB 5 duration 2.5

    erase tan_E l_cc

B: Then let Fi (@draw t_taste@)nn care less for fish than Mia does. The curve bends away from the middle, and the lens opens again.
    animate finn_fish 0.2 duration 2.5

Try it: drag Finn's taste for fish, and watch the lens open and close around equal parcels.
    explore params ["finn_fish"]

## Do people trade that far?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Edgeworth would sign that contract.
    good: Somewhere on the contract curve.
    poor: Still inside the lens. Swap again.
    none: Keep your mug, then.
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 10
    'y':
      - 0
      - 10
    box:
      x: 150
      'y': 168
      w: 475
      h: 475
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - given a mug
      - given chocolate
    values:
      - 89
      - 90
    icons:
      - {of: hot beverage, set: twemoji}
      - chocolate bar
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: '% who kept it'
    colors:
      - orange
      - orange
    reference:
      value: 50
      label: half
    label_size: 24
    gap: 0.5
    box:
      x: 50
      'y': 170
      w: 560
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: knetsch1989
      title: The Endowment Effect and Evidence of Nonreversible Indifference Curves
      authors: J. L. Knetsch
      year: 1989
      finding: Of students given a mug, 89% kept it rather than swap for a chocolate bar; of those given chocolate, 90% kept it; with no endowment, 56% chose the mug. American Economic Review 79(5).
    - id: plottzeiler2007
      title: Exchange Asymmetries Incorrectly Interpreted as Evidence of Endowment Effect Theory and Prospect Theory?
      authors: C. R. Plott and K. Zeiler
      year: 2007
      finding: 'Modified experimental procedures removed the exchange asymmetry; the traditional procedures brought it back. American Economic Review 97(4): 1449–1466.'
    - id: list2003
      title: Does Market Experience Eliminate Market Anomalies?
      authors: J. A. List
      year: 2003
      finding: 'At sports-card shows, inexperienced traders showed an endowment effect; traders with intense market experience showed little or none. Quarterly Journal of Economics 118(1): 41–71.'

    curve edge_b #3d3833 expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_t #3d3833 expr "10" x_from 0 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_l #3d3833 x_expr "0" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve edge_r #3d3833 x_expr "10" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    dot oM #2f6b8f at.x 0 at.y 0 hidden true
    text l_oM "Mia" #2f6b8f at.data [-0.7,-0.6] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot oF #b5482e at.x 10 at.y 10 hidden true
    label l_oF "Finn" above-right #b5482e attach_to oF hidden true
    text t_mf "Mia's fish →" #2f6b8f at.data [5,-0.75] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_mb "Mia's\nloaves ↑" #2f6b8f at.data [-1.3,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_ff "← Finn's fish" #b5482e at.data [5,10.7] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_fb "Finn's\nloaves ↓" #b5482e at.data [11.3,5] font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_ee "endowment effect?" x 815 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text t_pz "Plott & Zeiler:\ngap vanishes with\nother procedures" #8f887c cites ["plottzeiler2007"] x 815 y 440 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_list "List: hardly any\nin experienced\ncard traders" #8f887c cites ["list2003"] x 815 y 310 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c1 "Trade can reach\nefficiency:\nCONSENSUS" #4f7d3a x 815 y 500 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c2 "People trade\nthat far:\nCONTESTED" #b5482e x 815 y 330 font_size 30 hidden true
    curve bx_mia #2f6b8f expr 16/x x_from 1.6 x_to 10 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    curve bx_finn #b5482e expr 10-16/(10-x) x_from 0 x_to 8.4 style.stroke_width 2.5 hidden true
    region bx_lens x_from 2 x_to 8 between ["bx_finn","bx_mia"] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    dot bx_E #3d3833 at.x 2 at.y 8 hidden true
    label bx_lE "start" above-right attach_to bx_E hidden true
    curve bx_cc thick #8a5fa8 expr x x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    curve bx_core #b8860b expr x x_from 4 x_to 6 style.stroke_width 9 hidden true
    text t_next "many efficient\nendings:\nwho picks one?" #8a5fa8 x 815 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mth work [{"tex":"\\text{efficient: } MRS_{Mia} = MRS_{Finn}"},{"tex":"U = \\sqrt{F\\,B} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; MRS = \\frac{B}{F}"},{"tex":"\\frac{B}{F} = \\frac{10-B}{10-F} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; B = F"}] x 500 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Do people trade that far?"

B: So the box says: wherever tastes differ, a swap is waiting. Do real people take it?
    draw axes

The famous coffee-mug experiments had a swap ve (@draw t_ee@)rsion: in 1989 Jack Knetsch handed students either a mug or a chocolate bar, then offered each a straight swap.

B: When a third group simply chose, it split fairly evenly: fifty-six percent took the mug. So you'd expect about half to swap.
    draw reference_1

    ask "What share of the students handed a mug kept it rather than swap? Drag the bar." on bar_1 store g gap false
        right: You said {g:0}; it was {g.true:0}.
        wrong: You said {g:0}; it was {g.true:0}: far more than half.

Eighty-nine percent of those handed a mug kept it. And ninety percent (@draw bar_2@) of those handed chocolate kept the chocolate.
    highlight bar_1

B: Owning a thing seems to make it worth more to you. If that endowment effect is real, people stop trading well inside the lens.
    highlight t_ee

But is it? Charles Plott and Kathryn Zeiler found the gap shrank or vanished when they changed how the goods were handed out and chosen.
    draw t_pz

B: And John List found that experienced traders at sports-card shows hardly showed it at all.
    draw t_list

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 reference_1 t_ee t_pz t_list

    draw edge_b edge_l edge_t edge_r oM l_oM oF l_oF t_mf t_mb t_ff t_fb bx_mia bx_finn bx_lens bx_E bx_lE bx_cc

So how sure are we? That two people can trade their way to an efficient deal, and that there are many such deals, is consensus: it's logic. That rea (@draw c2@)l people go all the way is contested.
    draw bx_core c1

    fade edge_b edge_l edge_t edge_r oM l_oM oF l_oF t_mf t_mb t_ff t_fb bx_mia bx_finn bx_lens bx_E bx_lE bx_cc bx_core c1 c2 to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Want the math behind the contract curve?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

Where the curves touch, their slopes match: the loaves each would give up for one more fish, the marginal rate of substitution.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

B: With these tastes, utility is the square root of fish times loaves, so that rate is loaves over fish.
    draw mth_line_2

Set Mia's equal to Finn's, with the box's ten and ten, and you get loaves equal to fish: the diagonal.
    draw mth_line_3

    erase mth

@test

    erase mathq

    fade edge_b edge_l edge_t edge_r oM l_oM oF l_oF t_mf t_mb t_ff t_fb bx_mia bx_finn bx_lens bx_E bx_lE bx_cc bx_core c1 c2 to 1

    quiz "Two neighbours, one growing only tomatoes and the other only beans, swap until neither can gain without the other losing. What have they reached?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: A Pareto-efficient deal: a point on their contract curve. Not necessarily fair, and not the only one.
        wrong: Read the words 'without the other losing' again: here, a Pareto-efficient deal.
        + A Pareto-efficient deal
        * The fairest possible deal
        * The one deal a market would pick

    quiz "Two traders reach a deal where any further swap would hurt one of them. Is that deal fair?"
        right: Not necessarily: efficient only means no swap is left that helps both, and the contract curve runs right into the corners.
        wrong: The contract curve runs all the way into the corners: so, not necessarily.
        + Not necessarily
        * Yes: efficient means fair
        * No: efficient deals never are

    quiz "A buyer and a seller haggle over a used bike. Any price from $80 to $120 leaves both better off. Which prices give an efficient outcome?" voice b
        right: Any price in that range: the bike ends up with whoever values it more, and the price only splits the gain. Many efficient outcomes, again.
        wrong: Does the price change who ends up with the bike? Here, any price in the range.
        + Any price in that range, if the bike changes hands
        * Only $100, the middle
        * Only $120, the seller's best

So can two people trade their way to efficiency? Yes, if they keep swapping until their curves touch.
    highlight bx_cc

But the box gives them a who (@draw t_next@)le curve of efficient endings, and only bargaining and t (@highlight bx_core@)he starting point pick one. Whether a market of many people can pick it for them is the next question.
    erase c1 c2

